    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Developed in Havana, Cuba in the 1940s as a simplified, casino-banked version of Baccarat. It removed the rotating banker and player choices found in Chemin de Fer.',
      originCountries: [
        'Cuba',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1950s: Tommy Renzoni discovers the game in the Capri Casino, Havana.',
        '1959: Renzoni introduces the game to the Dunes Hotel in Las Vegas.',
        "1970s: Becomes the flagship 'high-limit' game for American casinos.",
        "2000s: Explodes in popularity in Macau, becoming the world's highest-grossing casino game.",
      ],
      evolution: "The transition from 'players banking each other' to 'house banking everyone' allowed the game to scale and be offered at much faster speeds.",
      cultural: 'In Macau, the game is played with extreme intensity, often involving players squeezing the cards (peeking) before revealing them, even though the result is predetermined by the deal.',
    },
