          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Predict which of the two hands (Player or Banker) will have a total closest to 9.',
          gameplay: "1. Players place wagers on Player, Banker, or Tie. 2. Dealer deals two cards to Player and two to Banker. 3. If either has 8 or 9 (natural), both stand and hands are compared. 4. Otherwise: Player draws on 0-5, stands on 6-7. 5. Banker follows fixed Third Card Matrix based on Player's third card (if any). 6. Compare final totals; closest to 9 wins. Tie returns all wagers. Banker wins: 1:1 minus 5% commission.",
          keyRules: [
            'The Banker bet wins slightly more often than the Player bet.',
            'Tie bets pay 8:1 (or 9:1 in some venues).',
            'Winning Banker bets are subject to a 5% commission payable to the house.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Bet on the Banker. Even with the 5% commission, it has a lower house edge (1.06%) compared to the Player bet (1.24%).',
          intermediate: 'Do not bet on the Tie. The Tie bet has a house edge of over 14%, which is mathematically disastrous over time.',
          advanced: "Disregard 'Roadmaps' (patterns). While many players look for 'streaks' in scorecards (Big Eye Boy, Small Road), each hand is an independent event with nearly fixed odds.",
          tips: [
            "Avoid 'system' bets like Martingale; the house limits and the commission will erode your edge instantly.",
            "If you want to play for longer, look for 'Commission-Free' Baccarat tables where Banker pays 1:1 except on a Banker win with 6 (which pays 1:2).",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
